dp-desktop-app — Release Artifacts and Verification
==================================================

This repository publishes the dp-desktop-app as an executable Java application
via GitHub Releases.

Each release corresponds to a Git tag of the form:

  rel-<version>

Example:
  rel-1.12.0-rc8

------------------------------------------------------------
Release Contents
------------------------------------------------------------

Each release contains:

  dp-desktop-app-<version>.jar
    Executable desktop application JAR.

  SHA256SUMS
    SHA-256 checksum for the JAR above, in sha256sum format.

  SHA256SUMS.cosign.bundle
    A keyless Sigstore signature over SHA256SUMS.

Releases through 1.16.0 instead shipped a separate
dp-desktop-app-<version>.jar.sha256, and were not signed.  That file recorded the
path as release/dp-desktop-app-<version>.jar, so `sha256sum -c` fails on it in a
flat download directory unless a release/ subdirectory is recreated.  The
verification steps below do not apply to those releases.

------------------------------------------------------------
Download and Installation
------------------------------------------------------------

1. Download the JAR, SHA256SUMS, and SHA256SUMS.cosign.bundle from the GitHub
   Release page into a single directory, with no subdirectories.  The commands
   below expect to find the files beside SHA256SUMS.

2. Verify the checksum and the signature.

   a. The checksum.

        Linux:    sha256sum -c SHA256SUMS
        macOS:    shasum -a 256 -c SHA256SUMS

      The output should indicate:

        dp-desktop-app-<version>.jar: OK

      Windows (PowerShell) has no equivalent of -c, so this one-liner compares
      the hash itself and prints True or False:

        $f='dp-desktop-app-<version>.jar'; (Get-FileHash -Algorithm SHA256 $f).Hash -eq ((Select-String -Path SHA256SUMS -SimpleMatch $f).Line -split '\s+')[0]

      Anything other than True means the JAR does not match: do not run it.

      SHA256SUMS lists only the JAR.  It does not list itself or
      SHA256SUMS.cosign.bundle, so this step says nothing about either file; the
      signature check below is what covers them.

   b. The signature.

      The checksum establishes integrity but not origin: it is published to the
      same release page as the JAR, so anyone able to replace the JAR could
      replace its checksum alongside it.  The Sigstore signature closes that gap
      by binding SHA256SUMS to the repository, workflow file, tag, and source
      commit that produced it.  There is no public key to fetch or trust: the
      signing identity is a short-lived certificate issued to the GitHub Actions
      run and recorded in the public Rekor transparency log.

      Install cosign v3.1.3 or later (binaries exist for Linux, macOS, and
      Windows):

        https://docs.sigstore.dev/cosign/system_config/installation/

      Check with `cosign version`.  Earlier versions have a verification flaw
      (GHSA-fx35-mq7g-6g98) that lets a substituted bundle skip the identity
      checks below, which defeats the point of this step.

      Then, with <version> replaced by the release you downloaded (e.g. 1.17.0):

        cosign verify-blob \
          --bundle SHA256SUMS.cosign.bundle \
          --certificate-identity 'https://github.com/osprey-dcs/dp-desktop-app/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/tags/rel-<version>' \
          --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com \
          --certificate-github-workflow-trigger push \
          SHA256SUMS

      The same command on one line (for Windows, where \ does not continue a
      line):

        cosign verify-blob --bundle SHA256SUMS.cosign.bundle --certificate-identity "https://github.com/osprey-dcs/dp-desktop-app/.github/workflows/release.yml@refs/tags/rel-<version>" --certificate-oidc-issuer https://token.actions.githubusercontent.com --certificate-github-workflow-trigger push SHA256SUMS

      The output should be:

        Verified OK

      Keep every identity flag exactly as written; each one closes a specific gap:

        --certificate-identity  is an exact match on this repository, the
            release.yml workflow file, and the one tag you are verifying.  A
            pattern matching any rel- tag would accept an older release's genuine
            SHA256SUMS and bundle substituted for this one's.  A loosened or
            unanchored pattern would accept a valid Sigstore signature made by any
            workflow in any repository -- the usual way this check is made vacuous
            while still appearing to pass.

        --certificate-github-workflow-trigger push  requires the signature to come
            from the tag push that published the release, not from a manual run of
            the same workflow, which would carry the same identity.

      The identity names dp-desktop-app.  dp-grpc and dp-service publish
      near-identical instructions with their own repository in the identity; a
      command copied from either fails against this release's bundle.

3. Run the application:

   java -jar dp-desktop-app-<version>.jar

------------------------------------------------------------
Configuration
------------------------------------------------------------

The application is typically configured via external YAML files supplied by the
data-platform installer.

------------------------------------------------------------
Notes
------------------------------------------------------------

- dp-desktop-app communicates with dp-service at runtime.
- It is normally distributed as part of the Data Platform installer.
